.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_CHECK_CHAIN 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_check_chain
.Nd check certificate chain suitability
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_check_chain
.Fa "SSL *s"
.Fa "X509 *x"
.Fa "EVP_PKEY *pk"
.Fa "STACK_OF(X509) *chain"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn SSL_check_chain
checks whether certificate
.Fa x ,
private key
.Fa pk
and certificate chain
.Fa chain
is suitable for use with the current session
.Fa s .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_check_chain
returns a bitmap of flags indicating the validity of the chain.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_VALID :
the chain can be used with the current session.
If this flag is
.Sy not
set then the certificate will never be used even if the application
tries to set it because it is inconsistent with the peer preferences.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_SIGN :
the EE key can be used for signing.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_EE_SIGNATURE :
the signature algorithm of the EE certificate is acceptable.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_CA_SIGNATURE :
the signature algorithms of all CA certificates are acceptable.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_EE_PARAM :
the parameters of the end entity certificate are acceptable (e.g.
it is a supported curve).
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_CA_PARAM :
the parameters of all CA certificates are acceptable.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_EXPLICIT_SIGN :
the end entity certificate algorithm can be used explicitly for signing
(i.e.
it is mentioned in the signature algorithms extension).
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_ISSUER_NAME :
the issuer name is acceptable.
This is only meaningful for client authentication.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_CERT_TYPE :
the certificate type is acceptable.
Only meaningful for client authentication.
.Pp
.Sy CERT_PKEY_SUITEB :
chain is suitable for Suite B use.
.Sh NOTES
.Fn SSL_check_chain
must be called in servers after a client hello message or in clients
after a certificate request message.
It will typically be called in the certificate callback.
.Pp
An application wishing to support multiple certificate chains may call
this function on each chain in turn: starting with the one it considers
the most secure.
It could then use the chain of the first set which returns suitable
flags.
.Pp
As a minimum the flag
.Sy CERT_PKEY_VALID
must be set for a chain to be usable.
An application supporting multiple chains with different CA signature
algorithms may also wish to check
.Sy CERT_PKEY_CA_SIGNATURE
too.
If no chain is suitable a server should fall back to the most secure
chain which sets
.Sy CERT_PKEY_VALID .
.Pp
The validity of a chain is determined by checking if it matches a
supported signature algorithm, supported curves and in the case of
client authentication certificate types and issuer names.
.Pp
Since the supported signature algorithms extension is only used in TLS
1.2, TLS 1.3 and DTLS 1.2 the results for earlier versions of TLS and
DTLS may not be very useful.
Applications may wish to specify a different "legacy" chain for earlier
versions of TLS or DTLS.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_cert_cb 3 ,
.Xr ssl 7
